Half the programs in [[textmode]] are ports — a flame, a maze, Conway, a word. They are
pictures of things, and you can say what they are in one word.

The other half are not. They have no subject. Something is iterated and what appears is a
consequence nobody chose, which is a different kind of image and the one I actually wanted.

## reaction–diffusion

Two chemicals on a wrapping grid. One converts the other; both spread; one is fed in and
one is drained off. Four constants:

    feed 0.078   kill 0.061   Da 1.0   Db 0.5

That is the entire program. Nothing in it mentions coral, or fingerprints, or the coat of a
big cat, and all of them come out of it depending on where you put those two numbers — so
they are a knob rather than something you have to go and edit. `bgopts rd=coral`, or
`worms`, `spots`, `holes`, `uskate`, `mitosis`, `solitons`. The field behind this page is
that rule, six iterations a frame.

> Two things cost me an hour each, and neither was code.
>
> The first was the Laplacian. With the obvious five-point one — up, down, left, right,
> minus four — the largest eigenvalue is −8, so `Da·dt·|λ|` is 8, the integration is
> unstable at `dt = 1`, and the field oscillates itself flat inside a few hundred steps. It
> looks *exactly* like the seeds dying. The nine-point kernel — `0.05` on the diagonals,
> `0.2` on the orthogonals — sums to magnitude one and is stable. Every working
> implementation uses it and almost none of them say why.
>
> The second only shows up on small grids. A pattern has a wavelength, the grid wraps, and
> if the grid is shorter than the wavelength the pattern meets its own wrapped copy and
> annihilates. `mitosis` is beautiful at three hundred columns and a dead blank rectangle
> at ninety. I measured all seven regimes from 40×12 up to 300×90; `worms` is the only one
> alive at every size, which is why it is the default and `mitosis` — the one I started
> with, and the one I wanted — is not.

## signed distance

A function that, given a point, returns the distance to the nearest surface. Walk a ray
forward by whatever that function returns, repeatedly, and you cannot overshoot — so you
arrive at the surface without ever having stored one. There is no geometry anywhere in the
program. A torus is:

    length(vec2(length(p.xz) - 1, p.y)) - 0.35

Shade it by the surface normal against one light, quantise to ten levels, and ten levels is
all a character ramp can say anyway. The mismatch that usually makes ASCII rendering look
cheap — the enormous loss between what was computed and what can be shown — stops mattering
when the thing being computed was never a photograph.

## subdivision

A rectangle splits. Its halves split. Six levels down it stops, and what is left is a plan
of a building nobody designed. Drawn as rules rather than fills, because the interesting
part is the boundaries and not the rooms.

It recomputes every six seconds and wipes in from the left. The wipe is the only part I
added for effect and it is the part I am least sure about.

## contours

Fractal noise, then `fract(v * 7)`. The noise is not the image. The *banding* is the image
— quantising a smooth field into a small ramp draws contour lines for free, and what you
get is a topographic map of somewhere that does not exist and never did.

## why bother

Because a background that depicts something competes with the text in front of it. A
background that is a process running does not. You can look at it or fail to notice it and
neither is wrong, which is the whole specification, and it is the reason the default one —
`drift` — is a slow fractal field with almost no contrast in which nothing whatsoever
happens.

[[background]] has the knobs. [[ascii]] has all fourteen of them running at once.
